# Back link

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A back link takes someone to the previous step of a task, with the answers they gave still in place. It suits a multi-step setup or form where changing an earlier answer is useful.

This page covers:

- Back link
  - Civica back link pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A back link is one line of link text with a small arrow before it. It sits above the page heading, after any page header and before the main content.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-back-link-pattern">Civica back link pattern</h3>

A Civica design with no Home Assistant element behind it. Write it as a link:

```html
<a href="#previous-rooms">Back to room selection</a>
```

Say where the link goes. “Back to room selection” is clearer than “Back”. Keep it in the same place on every step.

Home Assistant has its own back control in its headers, `ha-icon-button-arrow-prev`. It is an icon button with a translated label (“Back” by default), not a text link, and Home Assistant owns where it goes. Keep it as it is, with its label. Use the text link above in Civica pages and cards you build yourself.

There is no Civica theme variable for the back link. The link uses the theme link colour, and its focus follows the theme focus colour. Home Assistant decides the route, so a theme cannot add a back link to a screen.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A text link with a named destination is clear.
- It works with a keyboard and a screen reader because it is a real link.
- A deep link may have no previous step in the app, so there may be nothing to go back to.
- Browser Back and the link may go to different places. Do not assume they agree.
- You cannot turn the Home Assistant header arrow into a text link.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use it in a multi-step setup or form. Keep the destination predictable and keep the answers that are not secret.

Do not add one to every dashboard card. Do not use it with [breadcrumbs](breadcrumbs.html), and do not treat browser history as a guaranteed parent page. If going back needs to save an answer, use the form’s own submit button, not a link that changes something.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): the label names the destination.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order: it comes before the main content.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible: the theme focus colour shows on the link.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation and 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: same place, same words on every step.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: people do not retype answers after going back.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the link wraps at 320 CSS pixels.

<h3 id="check">Check it works</h3>

Test an edited answer, unsaved changes, a deep-linked page, browser Back and where focus lands after the return. Check light and dark modes, two theme colours and a 320 pixel width. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html). For moving between pages, see [Navigation](navigation.html).

